\section{Keeping the import cheap}

The package surface is not only a discoverability decision, it is also a
latency decision.  Host applications embed the CLI as a subcommand, so
[[import pytorial]] runs on every invocation of the host, whether or not
that invocation has anything to do with tutorials.  The CLI chapter
therefore loads its heavy dependencies at the point where they are used
rather than at module level (\cref{tutorial-cli}).

That kind of arrangement decays quietly.  A future contributor adds one
convenient module-level import, everything still works, and the cost
returns without a single failing test.  The tests below are what make the
decay loud: they assert not what the package \emph{does} but what it
declines to load.

<<test [[import_cost.py]]>>=
"""Tests that keep ``import pytorial`` cheap."""

import json
import os
import pathlib
import subprocess
import sys

import pytorial

<<import cost test functions>>
@

These tests cannot observe anything useful from inside the pytest process.
By the time a test runs, pytest has imported its own plugins, this test
module has imported [[pytorial]], and [[sys.modules]] holds a union of
everything anybody needed --- Rich among it.  The only place where the
question \enquote{what does this statement load?} has a meaningful answer
is an interpreter that has just started.

So each probe runs one statement in a fresh subprocess and reports the
module names that interpreter ended up with.  The snapshot is taken before
importing [[json]], so the reporting machinery cannot contaminate the
answer it is reporting.

We seed [[PYTHONPATH]] with the directory containing the package rather
than trusting the subprocess to inherit an import path.  The suite runs
against a working tree that may or may not be installed into the active
environment, and deriving the path from the already-imported
[[pytorial.__file__]] means the subprocess is guaranteed to import the same
copy the test process did.

<<import cost test functions>>=
def imported_module_names(statement):
    """Return the modules loaded by running `statement` in a new interpreter."""

    probe = (
        f"{statement}\n"
        "import sys\n"
        "loaded = sorted(k for k, v in sys.modules.items() if v is not None)\n"
        "import json\n"
        "print(json.dumps(loaded))\n"
    )
    package_root = pathlib.Path(pytorial.__file__).resolve().parent.parent
    env = dict(os.environ)
    env["PYTHONPATH"] = os.pathsep.join(
        path for path in (str(package_root), env.get("PYTHONPATH")) if path
    )

    completed = subprocess.run(
        [sys.executable, "-c", probe],
        check=True,
        capture_output=True,
        text=True,
        env=env,
    )
    return set(json.loads(completed.stdout))
@

The same fresh interpreter also proves that importing the package does
not require POSIX terminal support.  Shell steps defer those imports until
they can report the WSL hint; catalog commands remain usable on Windows.
Python's own [[subprocess]] optionally imports [[fcntl]] on POSIX, so
the first probe blocks that import as Windows would.  Entries set to
[[None]] represent blocked modules, not loaded ones; the harness omits
them.  The second probe blocks all four imports to check the contract
directly.

<<import cost test functions>>=
def test_importing_pytorial_does_not_import_posix_terminal_modules():
    loaded = imported_module_names(
        "import sys\n"
        "sys.modules['fcntl'] = None\n"
        "import pytorial"
    )
    assert {"fcntl", "pty", "termios", "tty"}.isdisjoint(loaded)


def test_importing_pytorial_without_posix_terminal_modules():
    loaded = imported_module_names(
        "import sys\n"
        "sys.modules.update(dict.fromkeys("
        "['fcntl', 'pty', 'termios', 'tty']))\n"
        "import pytorial"
    )
    assert "pytorial" in loaded
@

The first probe is the headline claim: importing the package does not pull
in Rich.  We collect every offending module name and assert on the sorted
list rather than on a boolean, so a failure names the module that
reintroduced the dependency instead of merely reporting [[False]].

<<import cost test functions>>=
def test_importing_pytorial_does_not_import_rich():
    loaded = imported_module_names("import pytorial")

    offenders = sorted(
        name
        for name in loaded
        if name == "rich" or name.startswith("rich.")
    )
    assert offenders == []
@

Importing the package is the cheap case, though, and a lazy package surface
could satisfy the first test while still loading Rich the moment a host
touches anything.  The second probe closes that gap by exercising the call
a host actually makes: registering the tutorial subcommand.  Registration
builds the whole command tree, so if any command body reached Rich at
definition time this would catch it.

The probe passes no tutorial paths, which keeps it from reading the
catalog; it measures registration alone.  Alongside Rich we name
[[markdown_it]] and [[pygments]] explicitly, because those are the
transitive costs that make Rich expensive, and a partial import that
loaded them without loading [[rich]] itself would otherwise slip through.

<<import cost test functions>>=
def test_adding_a_typer_subcommand_does_not_import_rich():
    loaded = imported_module_names(
        "import typer, pytorial\n"
        "pytorial.add_typer_subcommand(typer.Typer())"
    )

    offenders = sorted(
        name
        for name in loaded
        if name.split(".")[0] in {"rich", "markdown_it", "pygments"}
    )
    assert offenders == []
@

Typer is the other large cost, and the one that is easiest to lose track
of, because the CLI is \emph{written} in it.  A host built on Typer has
imported it anyway, but a host built on [[argparse]] never needs it, so
for that host the whole tutorial group must work without it
(\cref{tutorial-cli-deferred-typer}).  The cheap half of the claim is the
import.

<<import cost test functions>>=
TYPER_STACK = {"typer", "click"}


def test_importing_pytorial_does_not_import_typer():
    loaded = imported_module_names("import pytorial")

    assert sorted(n for n in loaded if n.split(".")[0] in TYPER_STACK) == []
@

The half that matters is that an argparse host can then \emph{use} the
group.  The probe plays such a host from registration to the end of a
run: it lists, runs a one-question tutorial to completion and reviews
it.  The run is the important verb, since its closing summary was the
last place where the argparse path reached for Typer.  The probe
captures the commands' output, because the harness reads the module list
from the same standard output.

<<import cost test functions>>=
ARGPARSE_HOST_SESSION = """
import argparse, contextlib, io, pathlib, tempfile
import pytorial
from pytorial.shell import ShellResult

LESSON = '''---
id: q
title: Q
summary: One question.
---

# Ask

```tutorial-step
kind: input
answers:
  - ok
```

Say ok.
'''

def answer(*, step):
    return ShellResult("2026-01-01T00:00:00+00:00", "2026-01-01T00:00:01+00:00", 0, "ok")

with tempfile.TemporaryDirectory() as directory:
    root = pathlib.Path(directory)
    (root / "q.md").write_text(LESSON)
    parser = argparse.ArgumentParser(prog="host")
    pytorial.add_argparse_subcommand(
        parser.add_subparsers(),
        state_root=root / "state",
        tutorial_paths=(root / "q.md",),
        question_runner=answer,
    )
    with contextlib.redirect_stdout(io.StringIO()) as output:
        for argv in (["list"], ["run", "q"], ["review", "q"]):
            args = parser.parse_args(["tutorial", *argv])
            assert args.tutorial_handler(args) == 0, argv
    assert "`host tutorial review q`" in output.getvalue()
"""


def test_an_argparse_host_never_imports_typer():
    loaded = imported_module_names(ARGPARSE_HOST_SESSION)

    assert sorted(n for n in loaded if n.split(".")[0] in TYPER_STACK) == []
@

Rich is the headline cost but not the only one.  Fetching a tutorial over
HTTP pulls in the HTTP client, the TLS layer, and the email package behind
the header parser, and nothing in a normal run of a host CLI needs any of
it.  We name all three, since importing any one of them means the fetching
stack came back.

<<import cost test functions>>=
def test_importing_pytorial_does_not_import_the_url_fetcher():
    loaded = imported_module_names("import pytorial")

    offenders = sorted(
        name
        for name in loaded
        if name.split(".")[0] in {"http", "ssl"}
        or name == "urllib.request"
    )
    assert offenders == []
@

YAML is the other one worth naming.  It is the format tutorial authors
write in, so the parser is unavoidable once a tutorial file is read --- but
reading a tutorial file is what [[list]] and [[run]] do, not what importing
the package does.

<<import cost test functions>>=
